# WhizMe Code Rules

- Language: Swift 6 (strict concurrency = complete). No warnings-as-noise; a new
  warning is a bug unless it is a documented deprecation with a comment saying why.
- Frameworks: SwiftUI, AppKit, Combine, and Apple system frameworks only.
  Zero WebViews, zero Electron.
- **Exactly one third-party dependency: Sparkle.** It is the sole exception and the
  bar for a second one is the same argument: macOS ships no update mechanism outside
  the App Store, so the alternative to Sparkle is not a system framework — it is
  hand-written privileged code that replaces the running app. Anything that *does*
  have a system-framework answer must use it.
  Sparkle is confined to `Services/UpdateService.swift`; no other file imports it.
  It is fetched and checksum-pinned by `Scripts/fetch-sparkle.sh`, never committed.
- Target: macOS 14.0+.
- Bundle identifier: me.whiz.app

## File separation — never combine responsibilities in one file

| Layer         | Directory     | Contains                                                        |
| ------------- | ------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------- |
| `Models/`     | data only     | Value types. Foundation only where possible; no AppKit, no IOKit. |
| `Services/`   | syscalls only | One system framework wrapper per file. Stateless or single-purpose. No `@Observable`, no UI. |
| `Managers/`   | state only    | `@MainActor @Observable final class`. Orchestrates services. Never calls a system API directly. |
| `Views/`      | pixels only   | SwiftUI views + AppKit windows. Reads managers, never a service. |

A new utility means: a `WhizFeature` case, a service per system API it touches, one
manager, and rows in `MenuBarView` / `SettingsView`. Nothing else should need editing.

`AppEnvironment` is the composition root and the single entry point for invoking a
utility (`trigger(_:)`), so the menu bar and the global shortcut always run the same
code path.

## TCC permissions

Always handle a denied or missing grant gracefully. Never let a utility fail silently:
set a user-presentable `lastError`, and route the user to the right System Settings
pane via `SystemPermission.settingsURL`. Deep-link URLs are declared once, on the model.

Remember that macOS shows each TCC prompt at most once per app identity — after that
the request call is a silent no-op and the only way forward is System Settings.

## Concurrency

- Managers and services that touch AppKit are `@MainActor`.
- `MainActor.assumeIsolated` is allowed only where a callback is documented to arrive
  on the main thread (Carbon hotkeys, `Timer`, main-queue notifications) — and the
  comment must say which.
- Continuations must resume exactly once. Take the continuation out of its stored
  property *before* resuming so a duplicate callback is inert.
- `@unchecked Sendable` requires a comment justifying it.
